# Catching Exceptions in JavaScript

## Basic `try...catch`

```javascript
try {
  // Code that might throw an error
  const data = JSON.parse(invalidJson);
} catch (error) {
  console.error("Something went wrong:", error.message);
}
```

## With `finally`

The `finally` block runs regardless of whether an error occurred:

```javascript
try {
  riskyOperation();
} catch (error) {
  console.error(error);
} finally {
  cleanup(); // Always runs
}
```

## Useful Error Properties

```javascript
try {
  undefinedFunction();
} catch (error) {
  console.log(error.name);    // "ReferenceError"
  console.log(error.message); // "undefinedFunction is not defined"
  console.log(error.stack);   // Full stack trace
}
```

## Checking Error Types

```javascript
try {
  someOperation();
} catch (error) {
  if (error instanceof TypeError) {
    // Handle type errors
  } else if (error instanceof RangeError) {
    // Handle range errors
  } else {
    throw error; // Re-throw if you can't handle it
  }
}
```

## Async Code

**With async/await:**

```javascript
async function fetchData() {
  try {
    const response = await fetch("https://api.example.com/data");
    const data = await response.json();
    return data;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error("Fetch failed:", error);
  }
}
```

**With Promises:**

```javascript
fetch("https://api.example.com/data")
  .then(response => response.json())
  .catch(error => console.error("Fetch failed:", error));
```

## Optional Catch Binding (ES2019+)

If you don't need the error object:

```javascript
try {
  riskyOperation();
} catch {
  console.log("Failed, but I don't need details");
}
```

## Key Gotcha ⚠️

`try...catch` **won't** catch errors in asynchronous callbacks:

```javascript
try {
  setTimeout(() => {
    throw new Error("Oops"); // NOT caught!
  }, 1000);
} catch (error) {
  // This never runs
}
```

For those cases, handle errors inside the callback or use Promises/async-await instead.
